 "circuits": [],
 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Configuration",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Four diodes form one full-wave bridge: the two AC leads each meet the anode of one diode and the cathode of another; two cathodes join at + and two anodes at -."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Uses",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "General-purpose full-wave AC to DC bridge rectification in monitors, televisions, printers, switch-mode power supplies, adapters, audio equipment and home appliances. Intended for printed circuit boards."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Polarity",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The lead functions are marked on the front of the case. The + lead sits below the chamfered corner."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Mounting and heat",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The 6.0 A rating needs the case bolted to an aluminium plate heatsink, held at TC = 90 °C; with a #6 screw through the case hole and silicone thermal compound under it, RθJC is 2.5 °C/W typ. Mounted only on a PCB with 12 mm x 12 mm copper pads and 9.5 mm leads, the rating is 3.8 A at TA = 40 °C and RθJA is 20 °C/W. Screw torque is 10 cm-kg (8.8 in-lb) at most, 5.7 cm-kg (5 in-lb) recommended. Output current falls linearly to zero at 150 °C on either mounting."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Isolation and safety",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The case withstands 1500 VRMS dielectric stress. The part is UL recognised under file E54214, and the molding compound is rated UL 94 V-0 for flammability."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Finish and soldering",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Leads are matte-tin plated and meet the solderability tests of J-STD-002 and JESD 22-B102. Both E3 and M3 finishes pass the JESD 201 class 1A tin-whisker test. The leads take a solder dip of 275 °C for up to 10 s (JESD 22-B106). E3 parts are RoHS-compliant; M3 parts are also halogen-free."
  }
 ],
